# Netlify redirects for lab.nexagenlabs.com
#
# The book prints thirteen URLs and this file is the contract. Every printed
# path has a rule here, nothing here is a path the book does not print, and
# tests/test_site_urls.py asserts both, plus the one that actually matters:
# that every destination is a path which exists in the repository. A redirect
# to a folder that does not exist is a dead link in a printed book.
#
# 302, not 301, everywhere. A permanent redirect is cached by the browser, so
# a reader who followed a printed URL once would keep going to the old
# destination after it was corrected, and would have no way to know. Print
# cannot be patched; this file is the only thing that can be, and caching it
# permanently would throw that away to save a round trip.
#
# There is deliberately no rule for /ch01 or /ch02. Those chapters ship no
# code, and inventing a destination for them would send a reader to the wrong
# build. Unmatched paths fall through to 404.html, which explains the position
# and offers the hub.

# The hub. A rewrite rather than a redirect, so the URL a reader typed is the
# URL they keep.
/                    /index.html                                                                200

# Chapter 0, setup. The clone, the virtual environment and the requirements.
/setup               https://github.com/nexagenlabs/agentic-lab/blob/main/README.md             302

# Chapters 3, 4 and 6 cover two builds each, so no single folder is the right
# answer: landing on one would hide the other, and a reader who never finds
# Build 02 has no way to know it exists. These three go to the builds tree,
# which lists all twelve in one view, and the hub carries the chapter to build
# mapping so a reader arriving from /ch03 knows to open 01 and 02.
#
# Single-build chapters are not made to follow suit. Uniformity would cost
# eight readers a step and a choice to spare three from an ambiguity they do
# not have. Where one folder is the whole answer, the printed URL goes there.
